1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,030
Imagine you live in the fourteenth
century. No internet. No television.

2
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:09,070
No newspapers. Most of the
people around you cannot read.

3
00:00:09,863 --> 00:00:14,653
One day a traveller arrives, and he says:
"In the north, a monster has appeared.

4
00:00:15,403 --> 00:00:18,833
It has the body of a man, the head
of a dog, and it eats children."

5
00:00:19,604 --> 00:00:23,844
How do you find out whether that is
true? You don't. There is no way.

6
00:00:23,924 --> 00:00:25,374
Maybe you don't believe him.

7
00:00:25,884 --> 00:00:28,614
But maybe, that evening, you
mention it to your neighbour.

8
00:00:29,645 --> 00:00:31,255
He tells a few more people.

9
00:00:32,025 --> 00:00:35,595
And within a week, half the country
knows there is a monster in the north.

10
00:00:36,421 --> 00:00:38,111
Today we would laugh at that.

11
00:00:38,521 --> 00:00:42,491
But were people in the past really
so much more stupid than we are?

12
00:00:42,721 --> 00:00:46,511
And did more nonsense actually
spread back then than it does now?

13
00:00:48,787 --> 00:00:51,197
People have always
believed strange things.

14
00:00:51,667 --> 00:00:55,657
A comet could be read as a warning
that something terrible was coming.

15
00:00:56,395 --> 00:00:59,135
And when the plague arrived,
the best medical minds in

16
00:00:59,215 --> 00:01:02,145
Europe concluded that the
air itself had gone rotten.

17
00:01:03,155 --> 00:01:04,745
That was not a fringe theory.

18
00:01:05,535 --> 00:01:07,105
That was the official answer.

19
00:01:07,951 --> 00:01:10,061
But most of those people were not stupid.

20
00:01:10,831 --> 00:01:13,421
They simply did not have
the information we have.

21
00:01:14,214 --> 00:01:18,234
And with no way to check anything,
a story changes every time it is

22
00:01:18,314 --> 00:01:22,664
retold, until it has almost nothing
to do with whatever actually happened.

23
00:01:24,841 --> 00:01:26,811
Of course, it was not only ignorance.

24
00:01:27,701 --> 00:01:30,111
Deliberate lies are as old as we are.

25
00:01:30,805 --> 00:01:32,235
Rulers needed propaganda.

26
00:01:32,585 --> 00:01:35,755
Politicians needed to justify wars.

27
00:01:36,565 --> 00:01:38,565
People spread rumours about their enemies.

28
00:01:38,645 --> 00:01:42,615
And during an epidemic, somebody
always had to be blamed.

29
00:01:43,451 --> 00:01:46,451
On the fourteenth of February,
thirteen forty-nine, the

30
00:01:46,531 --> 00:01:49,701
city of Strasbourg burned
its entire Jewish community.

31
00:01:50,951 --> 00:01:54,801
About two thousand people, according
to the city's own chronicler.

32
00:01:55,432 --> 00:01:58,922
The accusation was that they had poisoned
the wells, and caused the plague.

33
00:01:59,792 --> 00:02:04,452
It was a lie. The Pope had written,
months earlier, that the plague

34
00:02:04,532 --> 00:02:07,722
was killing people in places
where no Jews lived at all.

35
00:02:08,417 --> 00:02:12,647
It spread anyway. City after
city, for another two years.

36
00:02:13,507 --> 00:02:16,997
So, no. Disinformation is not
an invention of the internet.

37
00:02:17,907 --> 00:02:20,837
The internet only changed
its speed, and its reach.

38
00:02:23,491 --> 00:02:25,161
And then came new technology.

39
00:02:26,071 --> 00:02:29,421
The printing press could make
hundreds of copies of the same text.

40
00:02:30,223 --> 00:02:33,563
Historians estimate that around
twenty-eight thousand different

41
00:02:33,643 --> 00:02:37,733
editions from the first decades
of printing still survive today.

42
00:02:38,511 --> 00:02:41,241
Even at a conservative
few hundred copies each,

43
00:02:41,511 --> 00:02:44,001
printing had already
produced millions of books.

44
00:02:44,879 --> 00:02:47,309
Newspapers could inform a whole region.

45
00:02:47,819 --> 00:02:51,409
Radio and television could reach
millions of people at once.

46
00:02:52,273 --> 00:02:54,023
And the pattern kept repeating.

47
00:02:54,893 --> 00:02:57,623
The new technology made it
faster to spread knowledge.

48
00:02:57,913 --> 00:03:01,423
And just as much faster to
spread nonsense and propaganda.

49
00:03:02,507 --> 00:03:07,237
So the technology was not good, and
it was not bad. It was a multiplier.

50
00:03:08,127 --> 00:03:10,357
It multiplied truth and lies alike.

51
00:03:12,804 --> 00:03:14,854
And here is my favourite example.

52
00:03:15,804 --> 00:03:18,224
In nineteen thirty-eight,
Orson Welles put a

53
00:03:18,304 --> 00:03:21,434
radio play about an invasion
from Mars on the air.

54
00:03:22,161 --> 00:03:25,131
And it supposedly sent the
whole of America into a panic.

55
00:03:26,021 --> 00:03:27,711
You have probably heard the story.

56
00:03:28,481 --> 00:03:30,311
Millions of terrified listeners.

57
00:03:31,161 --> 00:03:32,931
People running out of their homes.

58
00:03:33,783 --> 00:03:35,533
Except that it didn't happen.

59
00:03:38,417 --> 00:03:41,637
The service that measured radio
audiences rang five thousand

60
00:03:41,717 --> 00:03:45,417
households that night and asked
what they were listening to.

61
00:03:45,497 --> 00:03:47,587
Two per cent said the Welles programme.

62
00:03:48,990 --> 00:03:53,180
And not one newspaper ever verified
a single death caused by the panic.

63
00:03:54,014 --> 00:03:57,094
The panic was reported by
newspapers, which at that

64
00:03:57,174 --> 00:04:00,204
moment were losing their
advertising money to radio.

65
00:04:01,107 --> 00:04:03,307
A story about the new
medium driving listeners

66
00:04:03,387 --> 00:04:06,577
out of their minds was
extremely convenient.

67
00:04:07,454 --> 00:04:09,854
So the most famous example
of a medium spreading

68
00:04:09,934 --> 00:04:13,184
mass hysteria is itself a
piece of disinformation.

69
00:04:14,394 --> 00:04:17,984
Spread by the older medium.
About the newer one.

70
00:04:20,490 --> 00:04:24,000
And then came the internet, and
that changed the rules completely.

71
00:04:24,938 --> 00:04:28,308
Somebody makes a claim?
You can look it up.

72
00:04:28,638 --> 00:04:32,648
Checking a fact has never in history
been as easy as it is right now.

73
00:04:33,337 --> 00:04:35,907
But the internet also
let everybody publish.

74
00:04:36,857 --> 00:04:39,407
Reaching a million people
used to need a newspaper,

75
00:04:39,677 --> 00:04:42,467
a radio station, or a television channel.

76
00:04:43,141 --> 00:04:45,171
Now it needs a phone and an account.

77
00:04:46,139 --> 00:04:48,039
So we got the largest library in history.

78
00:04:48,119 --> 00:04:53,969
And also the largest pub in history,
where anybody can shout across the room.

79
00:04:54,621 --> 00:04:58,371
And if it is shocking enough, a
million people hear it by the evening.

80
00:05:00,533 --> 00:05:02,343
And it doesn't even have to be a lie.

81
00:05:03,313 --> 00:05:07,443
Our brains love information that is
surprising, frightening or outrageous.

82
00:05:08,653 --> 00:05:11,983
So sensational nonsense can
travel further than boring truth.

83
00:05:12,750 --> 00:05:15,980
"Researchers analysed the
data and found..." Boring.

84
00:05:17,230 --> 00:05:20,600
"SCIENTISTS DISCOVER THE SHOCKING
TRUTH THEY ARE HIDING FROM YOU!" Click.

85
00:05:21,381 --> 00:05:24,991
And that is not just a
feeling. It has been measured.

86
00:05:25,721 --> 00:05:28,431
A study published in Science
followed a hundred and

87
00:05:28,511 --> 00:05:31,251
twenty-six thousand stories
spreading across Twitter.

88
00:05:32,063 --> 00:05:36,613
A false story was seventy per cent more
likely to be passed on than a true one.

89
00:05:37,443 --> 00:05:41,393
The truth took about six times as
long to reach fifteen hundred people.

90
00:05:42,230 --> 00:05:45,800
True stories almost never got
past about a thousand people.

91
00:05:46,590 --> 00:05:49,980
The most successful false ones
reached up to a hundred thousand.

92
00:05:50,819 --> 00:05:53,949
And then the researchers did
something that surprised everybody.

93
00:05:54,819 --> 00:05:56,769
They took the bots out of the data.

94
00:05:57,659 --> 00:06:01,609
The bots had been spreading true stories
and false stories at the same rate.

95
00:06:02,439 --> 00:06:06,109
The difference was not the
machines. The difference was us.

96
00:06:08,953 --> 00:06:11,003
So here is where it gets interesting.

97
00:06:12,033 --> 00:06:15,423
The problem in the Middle Ages
was not having enough information.

98
00:06:16,253 --> 00:06:19,623
Our problem is the opposite.
We have far too much.

99
00:06:22,047 --> 00:06:24,357
And now another large change is arriving.

100
00:06:24,807 --> 00:06:26,237
Artificial intelligence.

101
00:06:26,467 --> 00:06:29,257
The internet let us
search for information.

102
00:06:29,527 --> 00:06:31,677
AI can produce it.

103
00:06:32,387 --> 00:06:35,087
You can ask about a
historical event, a scientific

104
00:06:35,167 --> 00:06:37,917
problem, or anything you don't understand.

105
00:06:38,747 --> 00:06:42,217
And within a few seconds you
get a perfectly worded answer.

106
00:06:42,959 --> 00:06:46,789
But a perfectly worded answer
does not have to be a correct one.

107
00:06:47,279 --> 00:06:51,009
A model can produce a very convincing
claim that is simply wrong.

108
00:06:51,825 --> 00:06:53,235
And that is not all.

109
00:06:53,825 --> 00:06:58,015
AI can now also make images.
And video. And voices.

110
00:06:58,185 --> 00:07:02,975
So a false claim no longer has to be a
sentence typed somewhere on the internet.

111
00:07:03,851 --> 00:07:07,021
It can have a photograph.
It can have footage.

112
00:07:07,991 --> 00:07:11,581
It can have the voice of somebody who
supposedly witnessed the whole thing.

113
00:07:12,356 --> 00:07:16,886
It can even arrive with a very persuasive
assistant, ready to explain it all to you.

114
00:07:17,596 --> 00:07:19,166
And that is a new kind of problem.

115
00:07:19,856 --> 00:07:21,446
Not only: is this true?

116
00:07:21,796 --> 00:07:24,506
But also: who, or what, made it?

117
00:07:26,794 --> 00:07:30,064
So were people back then
more stupid? Probably not.

118
00:07:30,714 --> 00:07:35,184
A medieval person had far less knowledge,
and far fewer ways to check anything.

119
00:07:35,980 --> 00:07:37,710
We have the opposite problem.

120
00:07:38,540 --> 00:07:41,040
An enormous amount of
knowledge, an enormous amount of

121
00:07:41,120 --> 00:07:44,530
nonsense, and tools that
manufacture nonsense at scale.

122
00:07:45,379 --> 00:07:47,669
So perhaps we are asking
the wrong question.

123
00:07:48,519 --> 00:07:51,809
"Were there more lies in the
past?" A better question is:

124
00:07:51,959 --> 00:07:54,729
"How hard was it to find out
that something was false?"

125
00:07:55,589 --> 00:07:57,419
And there, the difference is enormous.

126
00:07:58,549 --> 00:08:00,349
In the Middle Ages you
heard a story from a

127
00:08:00,429 --> 00:08:02,939
traveller, and you had no way to check it.

128
00:08:03,602 --> 00:08:05,682
Today you can find the
sources, compare the

129
00:08:05,762 --> 00:08:09,392
accounts, and track down the
original data. In minutes.

130
00:08:10,290 --> 00:08:14,620
So perhaps we are not living in an age
with more lies in it than ever before.

131
00:08:15,378 --> 00:08:18,678
We are living in an age that holds
the best tools ever built for

132
00:08:18,758 --> 00:08:23,308
finding truth, and the best tools
ever built for manufacturing nonsense.

133
00:08:25,706 --> 00:08:27,146
Printing let us copy information.

134
00:08:27,226 --> 00:08:30,736
Radio and television broadcast it.

135
00:08:31,426 --> 00:08:33,296
The internet handed it to everybody.

136
00:08:33,586 --> 00:08:36,476
And AI has started producing it.

137
00:08:37,157 --> 00:08:39,097
Every one of those gave us something.

138
00:08:39,177 --> 00:08:43,647
And every one of them created a new
way for information to mislead us.

139
00:08:44,381 --> 00:08:47,951
So maybe the most important skill
now is not knowing everything.

140
00:08:48,481 --> 00:08:50,431
That isn't possible anyway.

141
00:08:51,327 --> 00:08:54,237
It is being able to say: "I
don't know. Let's check."

142
00:08:56,935 --> 00:08:59,015
Because when somebody tells
us today that there is

143
00:08:59,095 --> 00:09:01,335
a monster in the north
with the head of a dog...

144
00:09:01,415 --> 00:09:05,245
we don't have to believe it, and
we don't have to disbelieve it.

145
00:09:05,948 --> 00:09:08,478
We can just open the internet. And look.
